ructed from 3 snarks with 3-fold rotational symmetry on 28 vertices; one family has the property that the oddness of the family increases with m. The Flower Snarks {J}_{2k+1} found by Rufus Isaacs [15] can be drawn with -fold dihedral symmetry (shown in Fig. 1a; see [5], [23] for details), and [11].\n\nIn this paper, we develop new families of snarks that can be drawn with nontrivial geometric symmetry. We define a cyclic snark to be a snark that can be drawn with m-fold rotational symmetry for some . Every cyclic snark has the property that the automorphism group of that snark possesses an element which is cyclic of order m. Among known snarks of fixed order, those whose automorphism group has order greater than 2 are rare. Our general approach is to identify small examples of snarks exhibiting {\\mathbb{Z}}_{3} symmetry, interpret them as lifts of voltage graphs, and then use those voltage graphs to produce infinite families of rotationally symmetric snarks. While a common strategy for constructing snarks is to cyclically glue together suitably modified consecutive copies of a known snark (forming a “cyclic junction”), our techniques produce a family constructed from an initial non-snark, as well as examples that are rotationally symmetric but not formed as cyclic junctions.\nThe method requires the well-known Parity Lemma, which we state as follows:\n\nLemma 1.1 Parity Lemma\n\n[7], [23] If G is a properly 3-edge-colored cubic graph, whose edges have been colored 1, 2, 3, and N is a cutset of size n that contains {n}_{i} edges of color i, then .\n\nAlthough the term multipole is used in a more general setting (see for instance [14], [6], [20]), throughout\n\n### Snarks as lifts of voltage graphs\n\nIn 1976, Féodor Loupekine described (via R. Isaacs [16]) a construction for producing infinitely many snarks beginning with an initial snark, by removing a path {P}_{3} from a snark and observing that the resulting 5-pole had the property that if the left-hand semiedge